const e=[{id:"sv-5-526",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, how long can it take to run a kanban system through a **full supply chain**?",options:["About two to four weeks, the length of one kaizen event","About one quarter, once the card quantities are calculated","The same time as one DMAIC project, typically four to six months","Up to about seven years, based on published benchmarks"],answer:3,hint:"The book uses this benchmark to show that kanban is not a quick fix.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book warns that kanban is **not a quick fix** and quotes benchmarks of **up to seven years** to run it through a full supply chain."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Weeks, a quarter or one project length all understate the effort of building pull discipline across many suppliers."}],final:"Up to seven years across a full supply chain → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is the scale of a single kaizen event, not of a supply-chain kanban roll-out.","Incorrect — calculating cards is quick; the book stresses that the discipline across a supply chain takes years.","Incorrect — a Green Belt project length; the book quotes multi-year benchmarks for a whole supply chain.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-527",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the book, what is the MOST difficult part of running a kanban system?",options:["Keeping the discipline of a simple tool day after day","Calculating the correct number of cards for each loop","Buying software that can print and track the cards","Choosing the colour and layout of the kanban cards"],answer:0,hint:"Kanban itself is simple. What makes simple tools fail in practice?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that kanban is a simple tool; the difficulty is **maintaining the discipline** to follow its rules."},{title:"Apply",body:"Cards kept in pockets, work started without a signal and loops never resized are all discipline failures, not calculation failures."}],final:"The hard part is discipline → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — sizing a loop is a simple calculation; the book says the hard part is sustaining behaviour.","Incorrect — kanban can run on cards or empty bins; technology is not the difficult part.","Incorrect — card design is a minor visual-factory detail, not what makes kanban hard to sustain."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-528",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which observation shows that the discipline of a kanban loop has broken down?",options:["Empty bins are returned with their cards to the supplier","Each bin holds exactly the standard quantity on its card","Each card shows its number within the loop","Work is started at a step that holds no returned card"],answer:3,hint:"The book says the hard part of kanban is keeping its simple rules.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In a pull system nothing should be made or moved without a kanban signal; the book stresses that keeping this discipline is the hard part."},{title:"Apply",body:"Starting work without a card is push behaviour creeping back in. The other options describe a healthy loop."}],final:"Working without a card = broken discipline → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — returning empty bins with cards is the normal pull signal.","Incorrect — a standard quantity per card is what the book requires.","Incorrect — numbering cards helps the team notice a lost card; it is good practice.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-529",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Besides inventory, which two elements of waste does the book say kanban is especially good at attacking?",options:["Overproduction and waiting","Motion and correction","Overprocessing and conveyance","Correction and overprocessing"],answer:0,hint:"Think about what happens when a step works only on a signal from the next step.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book calls kanban the best inventory-control method for several of the seven wastes, especially **inventory, overproduction and waiting**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Producing only on a signal stops overproduction; a balanced pull flow reduces work waiting in queues."}],final:"Kanban attacks inventory, overproduction and waiting → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — motion is attacked by 5S and layout, correction by poka-yoke and root-cause fixes.","Incorrect — these are tackled by clarifying requirements and redesigning the flow, not mainly by a pull signal.","Incorrect — both relate to doing work wrongly or excessively; kanban controls when and how much work is done."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-530",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"How does kanban prevent **overproduction**?",options:["By scheduling each step to its maximum capacity every shift","By inspecting every unit before it moves to the next step","By authorising work only when downstream use sends a signal","By building a buffer stock in front of every bottleneck"],answer:2,hint:"What triggers work in a pull system?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A kanban is a **signal to begin work**. Without the signal, the step does not produce."},{title:"Apply",body:"Because the signal comes from actual consumption downstream, nothing is made that is not needed."}],final:"Work only on a downstream signal → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — producing to capacity regardless of demand is push behaviour and creates overproduction.","Incorrect — inspection finds defects; it does not control how much is produced.","","Incorrect — extra buffers add inventory; kanban caps it."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-531",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A team caps work-in-process with kanban while its throughput stays the same. What happens to the **average lead time**, and why?",options:["It rises, because fewer items are being worked on at once","It stays the same, because lead time depends only on throughput","It becomes unpredictable, because the cards slow the flow","It falls, because items spend less time waiting in queues"],answer:3,hint:"Little's Law links lead time, WIP and throughput.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Average lead time = WIP ÷ throughput. Lowering WIP at the same throughput lowers lead time."},{title:"Apply",body:"Work no longer waits in piles, so queue time — the largest part of lead time — shrinks."}],final:"Less WIP at the same throughput → shorter lead time → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — with throughput unchanged, fewer items in process means each spends less time waiting.","Incorrect — lead time depends on both WIP and throughput (Little's Law).","Incorrect — a WIP cap usually makes lead time shorter and more predictable.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-532",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Where does kanban sit in the book's Lean tools pyramid?",options:["At the top, resting on kaizen and every level below","At the base, as the foundation for all other Lean tools","Directly above 5S, as the second level","In the middle, alongside standardized work"],answer:0,hint:"Recall the order: 5S → visual factory → standardized work → kaizen → ?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The pyramid runs 5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → **Kanban**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Kanban is the top level, so it depends on every level beneath it."}],final:"Kanban is at the top of the pyramid → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the base of the pyramid is 5S; kanban needs the lower levels first.","Incorrect — the second level is the visual factory.","Incorrect — standardized work is the third level and must exist before kanban."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-5-533",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the book's Lean pyramid, which level lies **immediately beneath** kanban?",options:["Standardized work","Visual factory","Kaizen","5S"],answer:2,hint:"Kanban cannot be sustained without ongoing improvement events.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The order is 5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → **Kaizen** → Kanban."},{title:"Apply",body:"The book says kanban cannot be sustained without kaizen directly beneath it."}],final:"Kaizen lies directly beneath kanban → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — standardized work is one level lower, beneath kaizen.","Incorrect — the visual factory is the second level, just above 5S.","","Incorrect — 5S is the foundation at the base of the pyramid."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-5-534",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book list a **relatively stable demand cycle** as a condition for kanban?",options:["Because cards can only be printed once a year","Because stable demand removes the need for safety stock","Because kanban can only be used for make-to-forecast products","Because a loop sized for one demand level starves or overstocks when demand swings"],answer:3,hint:"The number of cards is calculated from an expected level of demand.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A kanban loop is sized for a demand level; the book lists a relatively stable demand cycle among the supporting conditions."},{title:"Apply",body:"Large swings leave too few cards in peaks (stock-outs) and too many in troughs (excess stock), which is why kanban is no quick fix for fluctuating demand."}],final:"Loop sized for one demand level → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — cards are cheap to reissue; the issue is that the loop size depends on demand.","Incorrect — even stable demand needs a safety margin for normal variation.","Incorrect — kanban replaces forecast-driven production with pull.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-535",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"The book calls kanban the best method of which kind of control for attacking several of the seven wastes?",options:["Cost control","Document control","Inventory control","Quality control"],answer:2,hint:"What does a kanban loop put a ceiling on?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes kanban as the best **inventory-control** method for attacking several of the seven wastes."},{title:"Apply",body:"By capping stock and WIP, it also attacks overproduction and waiting."}],final:"Kanban = best inventory-control method → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — kanban affects cost indirectly; the book's phrase is inventory control.","Incorrect — document control manages procedures and records, not material flow.","","Incorrect — quality is controlled by poka-yoke, SPC and inspection, not by pull signals."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-536",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On a kanban board with WIP limits, what does an **empty slot** in a column mean?",options:["The column may pull the next item from the previous column","The column is a bottleneck and needs more staff","The WIP limit for the column should be reduced","The team may start any new item from the backlog"],answer:0,hint:"On a board, capacity itself is the signal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An empty slot below the WIP limit is the same as the book's empty container: a **signal to pull** more work."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only that column may pull, and it pulls from the column before it."}],final:"Empty slot = signal to pull the next item → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a column constantly at its limit suggests a bottleneck; an empty slot means spare capacity.","Incorrect — one empty slot is the normal pull signal, not evidence that the limit is wrong.","Incorrect — work is pulled only into the column with free capacity, following the board's order."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-537",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book want the number of parts per kanban card kept **as small as possible**?",options:["To reduce the number of cards that must be printed","To allow each step to work in large economical batches","To keep inventory in the loop low and expose problems quickly","To remove the need for a stable demand cycle"],answer:2,hint:"Maximum stock in a loop depends on the quantity per card.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book requires a **standard** number of parts per card, **kept as small as possible**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Small quantities keep the loop's maximum inventory low, so delays and defects surface quickly instead of hiding under stock."}],final:"Small card quantities keep WIP low → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — smaller quantities need more cards, not fewer; printing cost is not the reason.","Incorrect — large batches are what kanban tries to avoid; they raise inventory.","","Incorrect — stable demand remains a separate prerequisite for kanban."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-538",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On a kanban board, an engineer has finished her card, but the next column is at its WIP limit. What should she do?",options:["Start a new card from the backlog so she is not idle","Push her finished card into the full column anyway","Raise the WIP limit of the full column by one","Help clear the work in the full column downstream"],answer:3,hint:"Stop starting, start finishing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In pull, work moves only when the next step has room. A full downstream column is the constraint."},{title:"Apply",body:"Helping to finish work there frees a slot so her card can move; starting new work only adds WIP."}],final:"Help finish downstream work → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — starting new work while downstream is full adds WIP and lengthens lead time.","Incorrect — that breaks the next column's WIP limit.","Incorrect — limits are changed deliberately by the team, not to absorb one finished card.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-539",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"After a project has cut stock levels in a spares store, which Lean control BEST holds the lower inventory in place?",options:["A kanban pull loop for each stocked item","A poka-yoke device on the picking shelves","An Xbar-R chart of daily issues","A one-off red-tag 5S event"],answer:0,hint:"Which Lean tool does the book name as the remedy for excess inventory?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book names kanban as the remedy for excess inventory; the number of cards caps stock in each loop."},{title:"Apply",body:"As a Control-phase Lean control, kanban sustains the reduced level after the project closes."}],final:"Kanban holds the reduced stock level → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — poka-yoke stops picking errors; it does not cap stock levels.","Incorrect — SPC monitors variation; it does not limit how much is held.","Incorrect — red-tagging clears clutter once; without a pull signal stock builds up again."],bookRef:"Book appendix p. 7"},{id:"sv-5-540",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Kanban puts which of Womack and Jones's five Lean principles into practice?",options:["Flow","Pull","Value","Value stream"],answer:1,hint:"Kanban is the technique used to move material through a Lean system on demand.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes kanban as the technique used to **pull** products and material through a Lean system."},{title:"Apply",body:"Of the five principles (value, value stream, flow, pull, perfection), kanban implements pull."}],final:"Kanban implements pull → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — flow removes stops between steps; kanban is the signal that regulates when work starts.","","Incorrect — defining value is about the customer's view of what they pay for.","Incorrect — mapping the value stream identifies the steps; it does not trigger work."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-541",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the ICGB syllabus, under which DMAIC phase is kanban listed as a Lean control?",options:["Analyze","Control","Define","Measure"],answer:1,hint:"Topic 5.1 is called 'Lean Controls'.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kanban is syllabus topic 5.1.2, under **5.1 Lean Controls** in the Control phase."},{title:"Apply",body:"It sustains gains by capping inventory and making flow problems visible."}],final:"Kanban is a Control-phase Lean control → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Analyze finds and verifies root causes; kanban sustains a process.","","Incorrect — Define sets the problem, scope and team; no control tools are installed.","Incorrect — Measure baselines the process and checks the measurement system."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-542",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On an IT team's kanban board, what does each card normally represent?",options:["One team member and that person's current workload","One column's WIP limit for the current week","One work item, such as a change request or ticket","One defect category from the team's Pareto chart"],answer:2,hint:"The card is the electronic equivalent of the book's card attached to material.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In service and IT work, each card is a **work item** that moves across the columns, just as a card travels with material."},{title:"Apply",body:"Columns show the steps; WIP limits on the columns cap how many cards each step holds."}],final:"Card = one work item → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — boards track work items; people pull cards, they are not cards.","Incorrect — WIP limits are written on the columns, not on cards.","","Incorrect — defect categories belong to a Pareto chart, not to a flow board."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-543",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Why does the book insist that ongoing **kaizen** exists before kanban is introduced?",options:["Because kaizen calculates the number of cards each loop needs","Because kaizen replaces the need for standardized work","Because kanban can only be used during a kaizen event","Because kanban exposes problems that must then be fixed quickly"],answer:3,hint:"What happens to hidden problems when inventory is capped?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kanban sits on top of kaizen in the pyramid; the book says kanbans cannot succeed without ongoing kaizens."},{title:"Apply",body:"Capping stock surfaces stoppages and defects quickly; without a kaizen habit those problems simply stop the line."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Kaizen neither sizes cards nor replaces standard work."}],final:"Kanban surfaces problems that kaizen must solve → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — card sizing is a separate calculation; kaizen is rapid improvement of the process.","Incorrect — standardized work is a level below kaizen and is still required.","Incorrect — kanban runs continuously; kaizen events are short improvement bursts.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 512"},{id:"sv-5-544",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Demand for a part in a stable kanban loop doubles after a permanent contract change. What should happen to the loop?",options:["Recalculate the number of cards for the new demand","Nothing, because a kanban loop adjusts itself to any demand","Remove cards, because higher demand empties bins faster","Switch the loop to a forecast-driven push schedule"],answer:0,hint:"The number of cards depends on demand, lead time, safety and container size.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Card numbers are sized from demand, lead time, safety factor and container quantity."},{title:"Apply",body:"A permanent shift in demand means the loop must be **recalculated**; otherwise it will starve or overstock."}],final:"Resize the loop for the new demand → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the number of cards caps inventory; a large permanent change needs the loop resized.","Incorrect — removing cards when demand rises would cause stock-outs.","Incorrect — a lasting demand change is handled by resizing the pull loop, not by abandoning it."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-545",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about kanban's place in the book's Lean pyramid is CORRECT?",options:["It is the first Lean tool to install because it is the simplest","It can be sustained only when the lower levels are in place","It replaces the visual factory once the cards are in use","It sits between 5S and standardized work"],answer:1,hint:"Each level of the pyramid depends on the ones beneath it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kanban is the top level: 5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban."},{title:"Apply",body:"The book states that without the lower levels kanbans cannot succeed."}],final:"Kanban depends on all lower levels → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says premature kanban will backfire; 5S comes first.","","Incorrect — kanban relies on the visual factory; it does not replace it.","Incorrect — that position belongs to the visual factory."],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 512"},{id:"sv-5-546",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about a **two-bin** kanban system is CORRECT?",options:["Both bins are refilled together at the end of each week","The second bin is used only when the forecast rises","The empty bin itself signals replenishment while the second bin is used","The bins may hold different quantities each time they are filled"],answer:2,hint:"In the simplest kanban, the container is the card.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says a kanban can be a physical signal such as an **empty container**. In a two-bin system the emptied bin goes back for refilling while work continues from the second bin."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Weekly refills and forecast triggers are push; varying quantities break the standard-quantity rule."}],final:"Empty bin = signal; second bin covers lead time → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a fixed weekly refill is a schedule (push), not a consumption signal.","Incorrect — the second bin covers demand during replenishment, not forecast changes.","","Incorrect — the book requires a standard quantity per card or container."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511, 570"},{id:"sv-5-547",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about kanban in IT and service work is CORRECT?",options:["Kanban cannot be used where there is no physical material","A service kanban board should be filled from the weekly forecast","A service kanban needs no standardized work because tasks vary","Column WIP limits on a board play the role of the cards or containers"],answer:3,hint:"What caps the work in each step when there are no bins?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"On a service board, each column's **WIP limit** caps the work in that step, just as the number of cards caps a material loop."},{title:"Apply",body:"A free slot is the pull signal; the lower pyramid levels, including standard work, are still needed."}],final:"WIP limits act as the cards → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the pull logic applies to tickets, orders and changes as well as parts.","Incorrect — releasing work from a forecast is push; items are pulled into free capacity.","Incorrect — the book's prerequisite of standardized work applies to service work too.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-548",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about kanban as a **Control-phase** tool is CORRECT?",options:["It sustains gains by capping inventory and exposing flow problems","It is used to find the root cause of excess inventory","It replaces control charts for monitoring the process","It raises safety stock so customers are never short"],answer:0,hint:"Why is kanban listed under Lean controls rather than as an analysis tool?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"As a Lean control, kanban holds the improved state: it limits stock and WIP and makes interruptions visible."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Root-cause analysis belongs to Analyze, variation monitoring to SPC, and extra safety stock is the opposite of kanban."}],final:"Kanban sustains gains by capping stock → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — finding root causes is Analyze work; kanban sustains an improved flow.","Incorrect — kanban controls flow and stock; SPC charts still monitor variation.","Incorrect — kanban caps stock; it does not raise it."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-549",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about the number of cards in a kanban loop is CORRECT?",options:["Adding cards always improves flow without side effects","The number of cards sets the maximum inventory in the loop","Cards can be removed freely without any stock-out risk","Once calculated, the number of cards never needs review"],answer:1,hint:"Each card authorises one container.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Every card authorises one container, so cards × container quantity is the **inventory ceiling** of the loop."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"More cards add stock, fewer cards add stock-out risk, and loops need periodic review."}],final:"Cards set the inventory ceiling → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — extra cards raise the inventory ceiling and hide problems.","","Incorrect — fewer cards lower inventory but raise the risk of running out.","Incorrect — loops must be resized when demand or lead time change."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-550",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about reducing the inventory held in a kanban loop is CORRECT?",options:["Removing cards is the safest way, whatever the lead time","Inventory can only be reduced by switching back to forecast ordering","Inventory falls safely when lead time, variation or container size is reduced","Inventory in a kanban loop cannot be changed once it is launched"],answer:2,hint:"The loop size depends on demand during lead time, a safety margin and the container quantity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Cards are sized from demand × lead time, plus a safety margin, divided by the container quantity."},{title:"Apply",body:"To hold less stock safely, shorten the lead time, reduce variation (and so the safety margin) or use smaller containers; then remove cards."}],final:"Change the process, then remove cards → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — removing cards without changing the process thins the buffer and causes stock-outs.","Incorrect — forecast ordering is push and usually raises inventory.","","Incorrect — loops are resized as demand, lead time or container quantities change."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-551",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the conditions for a successful kanban system are CORRECT?

1. A relatively stable demand cycle supports kanban.
2. Kanban is a good quick fix for poor planning.
3. Once kanban is running, standardized work is no longer needed.
4. The number of parts per card should be standard and small.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Check each statement against the book's list of supporting conditions and its warnings.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Supporting conditions include stable demand and a standard, small number of parts per card (1 and 4 true)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Kanban is not a quick fix for poor planning (2 false) and depends on standardized work (3 false)."}],final:"Statements 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 is false: the book says kanban is not a quick fix for poor planning.","Incorrect — statements 2 and 3 are both false; kanban needs standardized work and good planning.","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: consistent cycle times from standardized work remain a prerequisite.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-552",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the book's warnings on kanban are CORRECT?

1. Kanban is the best Lean tool to install first, because it is simple.
2. Benchmarks of up to seven years are quoted for a full supply chain.
3. The hard part of kanban is keeping the discipline of a simple tool.
4. Kanban is a quick fix for strongly fluctuating demand.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Compare each statement with what the book says about timescale, discipline and quick fixes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book quotes up to seven years for a full supply chain (2 true) and says the hard part is discipline (3 true)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Kanban is installed last, not first (1 false), and is not a quick fix for fluctuating demand (4 false)."}],final:"Statements 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: premature kanban will backfire; it sits at the top of the pyramid.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: the book says kanban is not a quick fix for fluctuating demand.","Incorrect — statements 1 and 4 are both false; they contradict the book's warnings."],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 512"},{id:"sv-5-553",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which statements about kanban boards with WIP limits are CORRECT?

1. Work is released to the board according to the monthly forecast.
2. A WIP limit may be exceeded whenever team members are idle.
3. A column that is constantly at its WIP limit points to the bottleneck.
4. A free slot below a column's WIP limit is a signal to pull work.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"A WIP limit is a pull rule, not a suggestion.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A free slot is the pull signal (4 true); a column always full is where work queues, the bottleneck (3 true)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Forecast release is push (1 false), and breaking limits to keep busy defeats the cap (2 false)."}],final:"Statements 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statements 1 and 2 are both false: forecast release is push, and limits are not broken to keep people busy.","Incorrect — statement 2 is false: idle people should help finish work, not exceed the limit.","","Incorrect — statement 1 is false: releasing work to a forecast is push, not pull."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-554",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book warns that if kanban is implemented too early, it will [?] and hurt the process.`,options:["accelerate","backfire","stabilize","standardize"],answer:1,hint:"The book uses a strong warning word for premature kanban.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says a kanban implemented before 5S, visual factory, standardized work and kaizen **will backfire**."},{title:"Apply",body:"The other words describe benefits or prerequisites, not the warning."}],final:"Premature kanban will backfire → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — premature kanban does not speed the process up; it disrupts it.","","Incorrect — stability is a prerequisite for kanban, not a result of introducing it early.","Incorrect — standardization must already exist; kanban cannot create it."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-555",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book calls kanban the best inventory-control method for attacking several of the seven elements of [?].`,options:["jidoka","muda","mura","muri"],answer:1,hint:"Which Japanese word means waste?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The seven elements of waste are called **muda**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Mura (unevenness), muri (overburden) and jidoka (stop on abnormality) are related Lean terms with different meanings."}],final:"Seven elements of waste = muda → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — jidoka is automation with a human touch: stopping when an abnormality occurs.","","Incorrect — mura is unevenness; kanban needs fairly even demand rather than curing it.","Incorrect — muri is overburden of people or equipment."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-556",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing words in the following sentence.

An incoming-material kanban is used to get material from a supplying department and regulates [?] between two processes.`,options:["defects","overtime","variation","work-in-process"],answer:3,hint:"A withdrawal card limits how much material sits between the two steps.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says Type 2 (incoming-material, withdrawal) kanban **regulates WIP** at a process."},{title:"Apply",body:"Material moves only when a card returns, so stock between the processes stays capped."}],final:"Regulates work-in-process → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — defects are controlled by quality tools such as poka-yoke and SPC.","Incorrect — overtime is a staffing decision, not something a material signal regulates.","Incorrect — variation is monitored by control charts, not by a withdrawal card.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-557",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

A kanban system needs a [?] number of parts per card, kept as small as possible.`,options:["forecast","maximum","standard","variable"],answer:2,hint:"Every card in a loop must mean the same amount.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Among the kanban conditions, the book lists a **standard** number of parts per card, kept as small as possible."},{title:"Apply",body:"A fixed quantity lets the loop be sized and lets anyone act on a card without asking."}],final:"Standard quantity per card → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a forecast quantity would turn the loop into push scheduling.","Incorrect — the book asks for small quantities, not the largest possible.","","Incorrect — varying quantities make the loop impossible to size and control."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-558",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT one of the conditions the book lists as supporting a kanban system?",options:["Improved changeover procedures","Consistent cycle times defined by standardized work","Trained material handlers","Large buffer stocks to absorb defective parts"],answer:3,hint:"Kanban needs near-zero defects sent forward, not cushions of stock.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists improved changeovers, stable demand, standard small card quantities, near-zero defects, consistent cycle times and trained handlers."},{title:"Apply",body:"Large buffers to hide defects contradict both the near-zero-defect condition and kanban's purpose."}],final:"Large defect buffers are not a condition → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this IS a listed condition: short changeovers allow small, frequent replenishment.","Incorrect — this IS a listed condition.","Incorrect — this IS a listed condition: handlers must move cards and material correctly.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-559",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"The book lists problems that kanban will not quickly fix. Which option is NOT on that list?",options:["Regulating WIP between two stable processes","Curing large inventory problems quickly","Resolving workforce issues","Compensating for poor planning"],answer:0,hint:"Separate kanban's real job from the problems it cannot cure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says kanban is not a quick fix for large inventory problems, workforce issues, poor planning or fluctuating demand."},{title:"Apply",body:"Its real job is pull control: regulating WIP between processes once the lower pyramid levels are stable."}],final:"Kanban regulates WIP between stable processes → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — large inventory problems ARE on the book's list of things kanban will not quickly fix.","Incorrect — workforce issues ARE on the book's list of things kanban will not quickly fix.","Incorrect — poor planning IS on the book's list of things kanban will not quickly fix."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-560",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which is NOT a name the book uses for **Type 2** kanban?",options:["Inter-process kanban","Intra-process kanban","Supplier kanban","Withdrawal kanban"],answer:1,hint:"Inter = between processes; intra = inside one process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Type 2 incoming-material kanban is also called withdrawal, inter-process or supplier kanban."},{title:"Apply",body:"**Intra-process** describes Type 1 finished-goods kanban, which controls production inside a process."}],final:"Intra-process is Type 1, not Type 2 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — inter-process IS a Type 2 name: it links two processes.","","Incorrect — supplier IS a Type 2 name: material comes from a supplying department.","Incorrect — withdrawal IS the main other name for Type 2."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-561",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"A kanban card normally shows all of the following EXCEPT:",options:["The part number and description","The operator's performance rating","The standard quantity per container","The supplying and consuming locations"],answer:1,hint:"A card carries what is needed to make, move or deliver the item.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A card carries production, conveyance and delivery information: what, how many, from where and to where."},{title:"Apply",body:"A personal performance rating has nothing to do with moving material."}],final:"Performance ratings are not on a kanban card → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the item identity IS on the card, so anyone can act without asking.","","Incorrect — the quantity IS on the card; the book requires it to be standard.","Incorrect — from-where and to-where ARE on the card to guide conveyance."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-562",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT a benefit of setting WIP limits on a kanban board?",options:["Shorter and more predictable lead times","A visible bottleneck where work piles up","Guaranteed 100% utilisation of every person","Less multitasking and more finished work"],answer:2,hint:"WIP limits optimise flow, not how busy each person looks.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"WIP limits cut queue time, reveal bottlenecks and reduce multitasking."},{title:"Apply",body:"They deliberately let people pause rather than start new work, so full utilisation is not a goal."}],final:"100% utilisation is not a WIP-limit benefit → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this IS a benefit: less work waits in queues.","Incorrect — this IS a benefit: the column at its limit shows where to improve.","","Incorrect — this IS a benefit: teams stop starting and start finishing."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-563",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'In a router-refurbishment cell, the test bench keeps working when it holds no kanban card, "so that people stay busy". Finished routers pile up in front of packing. Which kanban rule is being broken?',options:["Each card must show its number within the loop","Each card must carry a standard quantity","Withdrawal cards must regulate WIP between processes","No work is started without a kanban signal"],answer:3,hint:"What should authorise the test bench to start the next router?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A kanban is a **signal to begin work**; in its purest form nothing is made or moved without one."},{title:"Apply",body:"Working to stay busy, without a returned card, is push behaviour and creates overproduction in front of packing."}],final:"No card, no work → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — card numbering helps find lost cards; it is not what is being broken here.","Incorrect — the quantity per card is not the issue; the bench works with no card at all.","Incorrect — that describes the job of Type 2 kanban, not the rule broken at the bench.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-564",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A service desk adopts a kanban board, but each agent handles password, access and hardware tickets in their own way, so cards stall unpredictably. According to the book, what should be in place before WIP limits?",options:["Standardized work for each ticket type","A larger backlog column to hold waiting tickets","A control chart of tickets closed per day","A P.I.K. for each agent's work queue"],answer:0,hint:"Kanban needs consistent cycle times. Where do they come from?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists **consistent cycle times defined by standardized work** as a kanban condition."},{title:"Apply",body:"Agents working in their own way give erratic cycle times, so standard work must come first."}],final:"Standardized work before WIP limits → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a bigger backlog adds inventory; it does not make the work predictable.","Incorrect — SPC monitors output but does not create the consistent cycle times kanban needs.","Incorrect — a P.I.K. is a finished-goods production kanban; it does not fix inconsistent methods."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-565",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A depot launched kanban after 5S, visual boards and standard work were in place. The capped stock now exposes supplier delays and wrong picks every day, but there is no routine for fixing them, and managers start adding buffer stock again. Which level of the book's pyramid is missing?",options:["5S","Kaizen","Standardized work","Visual factory"],answer:1,hint:"Kanban exposes problems; which level is there to solve them?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kanban sits directly on **kaizen**; the book says kanbans cannot succeed without ongoing kaizens."},{title:"Apply",body:"The loop is doing its job by exposing problems, but with no improvement routine the team falls back on buffers."}],final:"Missing ongoing kaizen → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the stem says 5S was already in place.","","Incorrect — the stem says standard work was already in place.","Incorrect — visual boards were already in use before launch."],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 512"},{id:"sv-5-566",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'A project in a spares store is scoped to 5S and visual controls only. The Champion asks why kanban was left out, since "it is the tool that cuts stock". What is the BEST explanation?',options:["Kanban cuts stock only in manufacturing, not in stores","Kanban needs standardized work and kaizen in place first, so it comes later","Kanban and 5S cannot be used in the same area","Kanban is a Define-phase tool and does not belong in this project"],answer:1,hint:"Where is kanban in the pyramid, relative to this project's scope?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kanban sits at the top of the pyramid, above standardized work and kaizen; premature kanban will backfire."},{title:"Apply",body:"A project that only reaches 5S and visual controls has not yet built the levels kanban needs, so leaving it out is correct for now."}],final:"Kanban needs the higher levels first → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — kanban works in stores, depots and offices; the reason is its place in the pyramid.","","Incorrect — 5S is the foundation that kanban builds on.","Incorrect — kanban is a Control-phase Lean control (syllabus 5.1.2)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 512–513"},{id:"sv-5-567",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A provisioning team's kanban board, with WIP limits, is kept in a spreadsheet on the team leader's laptop. Engineers cannot see which columns are full and keep starting new orders. Which lower level of the book's pyramid is missing?",options:["5S","Kaizen","Standardized work","Visual factory"],answer:3,hint:"A pull signal only works if people can see it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The pyramid runs 5S → **Visual Factory** → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban; kanban relies on status being visible at a glance."},{title:"Apply",body:"A board hidden on one laptop gives no visual signal, so engineers cannot respect the WIP limits."}],final:"Missing visual factory → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 5S organises the workplace; the gap here is that status cannot be seen.","Incorrect — kaizen improves the process; first the team must be able to see the flow.","Incorrect — the problem is not how each order is done but that the board is invisible.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 508"},{id:"sv-5-568",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A change team's kanban board shows the **Verify** column at its WIP limit almost every day, while engineers in *Implement* often wait for space. Where should the team focus its next kaizen?",options:["On the Backlog column, to reduce the number of requests","On the Implement column, to make engineers work faster","On the Verify step, which is the bottleneck","On the Done column, to close changes more quickly"],answer:2,hint:"A column that is always full is the constraint.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A column constantly at its WIP limit, with upstream work waiting, is the **bottleneck**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Improving Verify raises the throughput of the whole board; speeding other steps only grows the queue."}],final:"Always-full column = bottleneck → kaizen there → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the backlog is not where work is stuck; the queue builds in front of Verify.","Incorrect — faster implementing would only add more waiting work in front of Verify.","","Incorrect — Done is the end of the flow; nothing waits there."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-569",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"In a cable warehouse, a painted square on the floor holds one pallet of cable drums. When the square is empty, the forklift driver brings another pallet from the store. What is the empty square acting as?",options:["A kanban signal","A poka-yoke device","A shadow board","An andon light"],answer:0,hint:"The book says a kanban can be a physical signal such as an empty container.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Anything that signals consumption and triggers replenishment — a card, an empty container, an empty floor space — works as a **kanban**."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Andon, poka-yoke and shadow boards are other visual controls with different jobs."}],final:"Empty space = kanban signal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — poka-yoke prevents or detects errors; the square triggers replenishment.","Incorrect — a shadow board shows where each tool belongs (5S), not when to replenish.","Incorrect — an andon signals an abnormality or a stopped process, not a replenishment need."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-570",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A Champion asks a Green Belt to introduce kanban to solve chronic staff shortages in a repair centre. The Green Belt explains that kanban will not solve this and proposes a separate workforce review. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because the book says kanban is not a quick fix for workforce issues","Yes, because kanban is only used in manufacturing, not in repair work","No, because kanban removes the need for staff by automating work","No, because kanban balances workload and so fixes staff shortages"],answer:0,hint:"Check the book's list of problems kanban does NOT fix.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says kanban is not a quick fix for large inventory problems, workforce issues, poor planning or fluctuating demand."},{title:"Apply",body:"Declining and redirecting to a workforce review is correct; option B gives a wrong reason for the right answer."}],final:"Appropriate: workforce issues are not a kanban fix → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — kanban works in repair, service and IT settings; the reason is wrong.","Incorrect — kanban is a signal for pull, not an automation tool.","Incorrect — the book explicitly lists workforce issues among the things kanban is not a quick fix for."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-571",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"In a repair cell, a Green Belt delayed the launch of kanban until 5S, visual controls and standardized work had been sustained for three months and kaizens were running. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because kanban needs the lower pyramid levels, or it will backfire","Yes, because the book requires a three-month wait before any Lean tool","No, because kanban should be launched first to expose the problems","No, because kanban and 5S are unrelated tools"],answer:0,hint:"What does the book say about kanban without the lower pyramid levels?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Without 5S, visual factory, standardized work and ongoing kaizen, the book says kanbans cannot succeed."},{title:"Apply",body:"Waiting until those are sustained is exactly right; the 'three-month rule' is invented."}],final:"Appropriate: lower levels first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — no fixed waiting period exists; the prerequisite is that the lower levels are in place.","Incorrect — the book warns that premature kanban will backfire.","Incorrect — kanban sits on top of the pyramid that starts with 5S."],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 512"},{id:"sv-5-572",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"Urgent outage changes keep jumping the queue on a change team's kanban board, so normal changes stall. What is the BEST response?",options:["Remove the WIP limits so urgent and normal work can both proceed","Let each engineer decide which card to pull next","Move urgent changes off the board into email","Add an expedite lane with a WIP limit of one"],answer:3,hint:"Keep urgent work visible but limited.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A separate expedite lane makes urgent work visible and gives it priority, while its own WIP limit stops it from swamping the board."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Removing limits, ad-hoc pulling and hiding work all break the pull rules."}],final:"Expedite lane with a limit of one → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — removing limits brings back the uncontrolled queues kanban was meant to cap.","Incorrect — ad-hoc choice hides priorities and lets urgent work disrupt everything.","Incorrect — invisible work defeats the visual purpose of the board.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-573",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:`A manager raised every WIP limit on a change team's board by 50% so that "nobody is ever waiting for work". Was this an appropriate action?`,options:["Yes, because higher WIP raises throughput and lead time falls","Yes, because WIP limits exist to keep every person fully utilised","No, because more WIP at the same throughput lengthens lead time","No, because WIP limits may only be changed by the Champion"],answer:2,hint:"Little's Law: lead time = WIP ÷ throughput.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Raising WIP without raising throughput makes work wait longer in queues."},{title:"Apply",body:"The change trades flow for the appearance of busyness, the opposite of what kanban is for."}],final:"Not appropriate: more WIP → longer lead time → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — more WIP at the same throughput lengthens lead time (Little's Law).","Incorrect — WIP limits exist to cap queues, not to maximise utilisation.","","Incorrect — the problem is the effect on flow, not who approved the change."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-574",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A plant manager wants kanban running with all 40 external suppliers within three months. What is the BEST response from the Green Belt?",options:["Explain it can take years, and pilot a stable internal loop first","Agree, and calculate card numbers for all 40 suppliers this week","Agree, but start with the 10 suppliers with the most variable demand","Decline kanban and return to forecast-driven purchasing"],answer:0,hint:"The book quotes multi-year benchmarks and warns against premature kanban.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kanban is not a quick fix; benchmarks of up to seven years apply across a full supply chain."},{title:"Apply",body:"Piloting one stable loop where the prerequisites exist builds discipline before extending to suppliers."}],final:"Set realistic expectations and pilot first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — quick sizing ignores the prerequisites and the book's warning about premature kanban.","Incorrect — variable demand makes kanban harder; the book wants a relatively stable demand cycle.","Incorrect — kanban is still the right long-term direction; it just needs a realistic plan."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-575",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"Kanban can succeed without 5S, a visual factory and standardized work, provided the number of cards is calculated correctly. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Is correct card sizing enough on its own?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: if you do not have 5S, visual factory, standardized work and ongoing kaizens, kanbans cannot succeed."},{title:"Apply",body:"Correct sizing is necessary but not sufficient, so the claim is false."}],final:"Correct sizing is not enough → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says that without the lower pyramid levels kanbans cannot succeed, however well they are sized.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-576",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put the levels of the book's Lean pyramid in order, from the **base** to the **top**:

- **K** — Kaizen
- **N** — Kanban
- **S** — 5S
- **V** — Visual factory
- **W** — Standardized work`,options:["S → W → V → K → N","S → V → W → K → N","V → S → W → N → K","S → V → K → W → N"],answer:1,hint:"Start with workplace organisation and end with the pull signal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Base to top: 5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban."},{title:"Apply",body:"Each level needs the one below it, so kanban is last."}],final:"S → V → W → K → N → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the visual factory comes before standardized work; it rests directly on 5S.","","Incorrect — 5S is the base, and kanban is the top, above kaizen.","Incorrect — standardized work comes before kaizen; kaizen improves an agreed standard."],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-5-577",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`A correctly sized kanban loop for ONT units has started running short. Put the Green Belt's investigation in a logical order:

- **P** — Count the cards actually in circulation
- **Q** — Resize the loop if demand or lead time has changed
- **R** — Compare current demand and lead time with the sizing data
- **S** — Monitor stock-outs after the change
- **T** — Recover any cards held outside the loop`,options:["R → Q → P → T → S","P → R → T → S → Q","P → T → R → Q → S","T → P → Q → R → S"],answer:2,hint:"Rule out discipline problems before changing the design of the loop.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"First check the loop as built (count cards, recover any held outside), then check whether its sizing assumptions still hold."},{title:"Apply",body:"Count → recover → compare demand and lead time → resize if needed → monitor."}],final:"P → T → R → Q → S → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — resizing first could add cards when the real cause is lost or held cards.","Incorrect — monitoring belongs after the corrective change, not before resizing.","","Incorrect — you cannot recover missing cards before counting them, or resize before comparing the data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-578",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put the steps for setting up a new kanban loop in a logical order:

- **P** — Calculate the number of cards for the loop
- **Q** — Select a part with relatively stable demand
- **R** — Audit the loop and resize it if needed
- **S** — Fix a standard, small quantity per container
- **T** — Launch the cards and train the material handlers`,options:["Q → P → S → T → R","S → Q → P → R → T","Q → S → T → P → R","Q → S → P → T → R"],answer:3,hint:"You cannot size a loop before you know the part and the container quantity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Choose a suitable part (stable demand) → set a standard, small container quantity → size the loop → launch and train → audit."},{title:"Apply",body:"The card count depends on the container quantity, and the audit can only happen once the loop runs."}],final:"Q → S → P → T → R → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the container quantity must be fixed before the number of cards can be calculated.","Incorrect — the part must be chosen first, and the audit follows the launch.","Incorrect — cards are calculated before they are launched.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-579",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A column on a kanban board reaches its WIP limit because one item is blocked. Put the team's response in order:

- **W** — Pull the next item into the freed slot
- **X** — Stop starting new work in that column
- **Y** — Swarm on the blocked item to clear it
- **Z** — Move the finished item to the next column`,options:["X → Y → Z → W","Y → X → W → Z","W → X → Y → Z","X → W → Y → Z"],answer:0,hint:"Stop starting, start finishing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"At the limit, the team stops starting new work, helps finish the blocked item, moves it on, and only then pulls."},{title:"Apply",body:"That order keeps the WIP limit intact throughout."}],final:"X → Y → Z → W → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — new work must stop first, and the slot frees only after the item moves on.","Incorrect — pulling new work into a full column breaks the WIP limit.","Incorrect — there is no free slot to pull into until the blocked item moves on."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-580",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Before launching kanban in a CPE repair store, a Green Belt completes a readiness checklist:

\`\`\`
Kanban Readiness Check – CPE repair store
Criterion                               Status
5S audit score (target >= 85%)          91%
Visual status boards in use             Yes
Standard work for all repair types      Yes
Kaizen events in last 6 months          3
Weekly demand, coefficient of var.      0.12 (stable)
Standard quantity per container         Yes (10)
Material handlers trained on cards      0 of 6
\`\`\`

Based on the checklist, which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The store is ready, because every pyramid level is in place","The store should train its material handlers before launch","The store should first repeat its 5S, because 91% is too low","The store cannot use kanban, because demand varies by 12%"],answer:1,hint:"Compare each line with the book's list of kanban conditions.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Every line meets its target except **material handlers trained: 0 of 6**."},{title:"Apply",body:"The book lists trained material handlers as a condition for kanban, so training comes before launch."}],final:"Only gap = untrained handlers → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the pyramid levels are in place, but the book's condition on trained handlers is not met.","","Incorrect — 91% exceeds the 85% target, so 5S is not the gap.","Incorrect — a coefficient of variation of 0.12 is marked stable, so demand is not the problem."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-581",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A depot reviews four parts as candidates for its first kanban loop:

\`\`\`
Part              Mean daily   StDev daily   Defects sent   Changeover
                  demand       demand        forward        (minutes)
ONT units          60           6            0.2%            10
Power adaptors     45          38            0.3%            12
Fibre splitters    30           4            6.5%            15
Custom enclosures   2           3            0.1%           240
\`\`\`

Which part is the BEST candidate?`,options:["Power adaptors, because they have high demand","Fibre splitters, because their demand has the lowest variation","ONT units, because demand is stable and few defects are sent forward","Custom enclosures, because they have the fewest defects"],answer:2,hint:"Check demand stability, defects sent forward and changeover for each part.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"ONT units: stable demand (StDev 6 on a mean of 60), 0.2% defects, short changeover."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Adaptors have erratic demand, splitters send defects forward, and enclosures have tiny demand with long changeovers."}],final:"ONT units meet all the conditions → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — their StDev (38) is close to the mean (45), so demand is far from stable.","Incorrect — 6.5% defects sent forward breaks the book's near-zero-defect condition.","","Incorrect — tiny, erratic demand and a 240-minute changeover make a pull loop impractical."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-582",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt reviews the Lean roll-out plan for a regional depot:

\`\`\`
Lean roll-out – Depot 5 (status at month 4)
Level                 Status       Month
5S                    Complete       1
Visual factory        Complete       2
Standardized work     Planned        8
Kaizen events         Not started    –
Kanban loops          Launched       3
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The plan follows the book, because 5S came first","Kaizen should wait until kanban has run for a year","Standardized work is not needed once kanban is running","Kanban was launched too early and is likely to backfire"],answer:3,hint:"Compare the launch month of kanban with the levels beneath it.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Kanban launched in month 3; standardized work is only planned for month 8 and kaizen has not started."},{title:"Apply",body:"Kanban sits at the top of the pyramid; launched before standardized work and kaizen, the book warns it **will backfire**."}],final:"Kanban launched before its prerequisites → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 5S came first, but kanban was launched before standardized work and kaizen.","Incorrect — kaizen sits beneath kanban and must already be running.","Incorrect — consistent cycle times from standardized work are a kanban condition.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 512"},{id:"sv-5-583",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A change team added an expedite lane with a WIP limit of **1** to its kanban board. Six weeks later the board data show:

\`\`\`
Expedite lane review – change board
Week                      1     2     3     4     5     6
Expedite cards (avg)      1     1     2     3     4     4
Share of changes marked   5%    6%   14%   22%   31%   35%
  'urgent'
Normal-lane lead time    4.1   4.3   5.2   6.8   8.9   9.6  (days)
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The expedite lane is being overused, and its limit and entry rules need enforcing","The expedite lane is working, because urgent changes are flowing","The lead-time rise is unrelated to the expedite lane","The WIP limit of the expedite lane should be raised to 4"],answer:0,hint:"Compare the lane's actual load with its limit, and watch the normal lane.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Expedite cards rose from 1 to 4 against a limit of 1, 'urgent' changes rose from 5% to 35%, and normal lead time rose from 4.1 to 9.6 days."},{title:"Apply",body:"An expedite lane only protects flow when its limit holds; enforce the limit and the criteria for 'urgent'."}],final:"Expedite lane overused → enforce its limit → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the lane has broken its own limit of 1 and normal work is slowing badly.","Incorrect — the lead-time rise tracks the growing share of 'urgent' changes week by week.","Incorrect — raising the limit would legitimise the abuse and slow normal work further."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-584",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt reduced the cards in a patch-cord loop step by step without changing lead time or demand:

\`\`\`
Patch-cord kanban loop – quarterly review
Quarter   Cards   Avg stock (cords)   Stock-outs
Q1         10          310               0
Q2          8          245               0
Q3          6          170               4
Q4          5          140              11
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Removing cards has no effect on how often stock runs out","Removing cards lowered stock but raised stock-out risk","Stock-outs rose because demand became unstable","The loop should now be cut to four cards"],answer:1,hint:"Each card authorises one container; what happens when the buffer gets too thin?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Average stock fell from 310 to 140, while stock-outs rose from 0 to 11."},{title:"Apply",body:"Fewer cards mean less inventory but a thinner buffer; to cut stock safely, shorten lead time or reduce variation rather than just removing cards."}],final:"Fewer cards: less stock, more stock-outs → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — stock-outs rose from 0 to 11 as cards fell.","","Incorrect — the stem states that demand and lead time did not change.","Incorrect — cutting further would make stock-outs worse; reduce lead time or variation instead."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-585",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A provisioning team introduced WIP limits on its kanban board in week 7:

\`\`\`
Provisioning board – weekly averages
Period         Avg WIP   Throughput/day   Avg lead time (days)
Weeks 1–6        48           6.0               8.0
Weeks 8–13       18           6.1               3.0
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Lead time fell because the team worked much faster","WIP limits reduced the team's output per day","Lead time fell mainly because less work waited in the system","Lead time is unrelated to the amount of work in progress"],answer:2,hint:"Compare WIP, throughput and lead time before and after.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"WIP fell from 48 to 18 while throughput stayed near 6 per day; lead time fell from 8.0 to 3.0 days."},{title:"Apply",body:"With the same throughput, less WIP means less queueing, so lead time falls (Little's Law)."}],final:"Less WIP, same throughput → shorter lead time → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — throughput barely changed (6.0 to 6.1 per day).","Incorrect — throughput stayed at about six orders per day.","","Incorrect — Little's Law links lead time directly to WIP at a given throughput."],bookRef:"Syllabus 5.1.2"},{id:"sv-5-586",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt plans kanban for a machining cell that makes four bracket types. Data from the last month:

\`\`\`
Bracket   Avg changeover (min)   Typical run size   Weeks of stock held
BR-10           180                 2,400                 5.5
BR-12           175                 2,000                 4.8
BR-20           190                 2,600                 6.1
BR-24           185                 2,200                 5.2
\`\`\`

Based on the book's kanban conditions, what should come FIRST?`,options:["Add more cards so that each run fills the loop","Calculate safety factors for each bracket type","Raise run sizes to reduce the number of changeovers","Improve changeover procedures so smaller runs are economic"],answer:3,hint:"Long changeovers force big batches. Which book condition addresses that?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Every changeover takes about three hours, so the cell runs 2,000+ pieces and holds five to six weeks of stock."},{title:"Apply",body:"The book lists **improved changeover procedures** as a kanban condition; short changeovers allow small, frequent replenishment."}],final:"Improve changeovers first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — more cards would lock in the large stocks the long changeovers create.","Incorrect — sizing comes later; the changeover problem must be fixed first.","Incorrect — larger runs increase inventory, the opposite of kanban's aim.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-587",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A new kanban loop between an assembly cell and its sub-assembly supplier keeps running short. A Green Belt reviews the data:

\`\`\`
Kanban loop review – sub-assemblies (4 weeks)
Cards in loop                    6
Bins received                   48
Bins rejected at assembly        7   (14.6%)
Stock-outs at assembly           5
Cards found outside the loop     0
\`\`\`

Which book condition for kanban is NOT being met?`,options:["Near-zero defects sent to the next process","A standard number of parts per card","Trained material handlers","A relatively stable demand cycle"],answer:0,hint:"What happens to a loop when bins arrive that cannot be used?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"7 of 48 bins (14.6%) were rejected, and 5 stock-outs followed; no cards were lost."},{title:"Apply",body:"Rejected bins consume cards without supplying usable parts. The book requires small variation and **near-zero defects** sent forward."}],final:"Defects sent forward break the loop → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — nothing in the output shows varying bin quantities.","Incorrect — no cards were found outside the loop, so card handling looks sound.","Incorrect — the output gives no evidence of demand swings; the shortages follow the rejected bins."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-588",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, what is a **Type 1 corrective action** (countermeasure)?",options:["A change that detects the error and shuts down the equipment","A change that eliminates the error condition so the defect is never created","An audit or 100% inspection that contains defects in the short term","A written procedure that tells operators how to avoid the error"],answer:1,hint:"Type 1 sits at the top of the book's control ranking.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: a Type 1 countermeasure **eliminates the error condition** — "the defect will never be created" — through mistake proofing or design change.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Detection with shut-down is Type 2; inspection is Type 3; an SOP is weaker still."}],final:"Type 1 removes the error condition → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is a Type 2 corrective action, the flag.","","Incorrect — that is a Type 3 corrective action, inspection.","Incorrect — an SOP alone is the least effective control in the ranking."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-589",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, what does a **Type 2 corrective action** (flag) do?",options:["It removes the error condition by redesigning the process","It sorts good from bad output at the end of the line","It detects the error condition and shuts down so the defect does not move forward","It trains operators so they notice errors more often"],answer:2,hint:"A flag does not stop the error happening, but it stops it travelling.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Type 2 flag **detects** that the error condition has occurred and **shuts down** the equipment so the defect does not progress."},{title:"Apply",body:"It is the detection use of poka-yoke, ranked second after prevention."}],final:"Type 2 detects and shuts down → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — removing the error condition is a Type 1 countermeasure.","Incorrect — sorting output is inspection, a Type 3 containment action.","","Incorrect — training depends on people and ranks below inspection."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-590",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book rank SPC on the X's, with trained and empowered staff, above 100% inspection of the output?",options:["Because SPC charts remove the error condition from the process","Because SPC finds every defect that inspection would miss","Because SPC needs no people to respond to its signals","Because SPC watches the inputs and lets staff act before defects are made"],answer:3,hint:"One control acts on causes as they happen; the other sorts results afterwards.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"SPC on the X's tracks the **inputs**, so empowered staff can react to a special cause before it produces defects."},{title:"Apply",body:"Inspection (Type 3) only finds defects after they are made, so it is containment and ranks lower."}],final:"SPC on X's acts before defects are made → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — removing the error condition is a Type 1 countermeasure; SPC only signals.","Incorrect — SPC does not sort output; it detects special causes in the process.","Incorrect — the book's condition is exactly that trained, empowered people respond.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-591",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the book's ranking, which control takes the long-term form of mistake proofing or a design change?",options:["Type 1 corrective action (countermeasure)","Type 2 corrective action (flag)","Type 3 corrective action (inspection)","SPC on the X's"],answer:0,hint:"Which type makes sure the defect will never be created?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says the Type 1 countermeasure is a **long-term** action in the form of **mistake proofing or design change**."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Type 2 detects, Type 3 contains, and SPC monitors."}],final:"Long-term mistake proofing / design change = Type 1 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a flag detects the error and shuts down; it does not change the design.","Incorrect — inspection is short-term containment.","Incorrect — SPC monitors inputs; it is not a design change."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-592",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Why does the book rank a **standard operating procedure (SOP) alone** at the bottom of its control ranking?",options:["Because SOPs are too expensive to write and maintain","Because an SOP alone is not sustainable: it relies on people following it","Because SOPs can only be used in manufacturing processes","Because an SOP stops the process too often"],answer:1,hint:"Ask what an SOP depends on to work.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes SOP alone as **not sustainable**."},{title:"Apply",body:"An SOP works only if people remember and follow it, so errors still happen under pressure or with new staff."}],final:"SOP alone is not sustainable → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — cost is not the reason; SOPs are cheap but rely on memory and attention.","","Incorrect — SOPs are used in every kind of process; the issue is reliance on people.","Incorrect — an SOP cannot stop anything; that is a feature of a flag."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-593",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"In the Control-plan pages, the book orders improvement types from strongest to weakest. Which type ranks **just above** training people not to make defects?",options:["Removing the root cause","Monitoring or flagging the error","Inspecting the output","Writing a new performance target"],answer:2,hint:"The order runs remove → monitor/flag → inspect → train.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: remove the root cause > monitor or flag > **inspect** > train people."},{title:"Apply",body:"Inspection sits just above training, which is the weakest."}],final:"Inspection ranks just above training → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — removing the root cause is the strongest type, at the top of the order.","Incorrect — monitoring or flagging ranks second, above inspection.","","Incorrect — a target is not one of the four improvement types and controls nothing by itself."],bookRef:"Book p. 557"},{id:"sv-5-594",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book rank a Type 2 flag above SPC on the X's?",options:["Because a flag removes the error condition permanently","Because a flag is cheaper to install than any control chart","Because SPC can only be used on outputs, not on inputs","Because a flag reacts automatically, without waiting for someone to read a chart"],answer:3,hint:"Which of the two depends on a person interpreting a signal?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Type 2 flag **detects and shuts down** by itself. SPC needs trained, empowered people to notice and act."},{title:"Apply",body:"The less a control depends on people, the higher it ranks."}],final:"Flag acts automatically; SPC needs people → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — removing the error condition is Type 1; a flag lets the error happen and then stops.","Incorrect — cost is not the basis of the ranking; dependence on people is.","Incorrect — the book's rank 3 is SPC on the X's, the inputs.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-595",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book still rank 100% inspection above an SOP on its own?",options:["Because inspection at least stops found defects reaching the customer","Because inspection removes the error condition","Because inspection is a long-term design change","Because an SOP stops the process too often"],answer:0,hint:"What does each control actually do when an error happens?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Inspection (Type 3) catches at least some defects before they leave, so it contains the problem."},{title:"Apply",body:"An SOP alone relies entirely on people following it, so the book calls it not sustainable and ranks it last."}],final:"Inspection contains; SOP alone only instructs → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — inspection finds defects after they are made; it removes nothing.","Incorrect — the book calls inspection short-term containment.","Incorrect — an SOP cannot stop a process; it only tells people what to do."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-596",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the Control-plan pages, which type of improvement does the book rank as the MOST effective?",options:["Inspecting the output","Monitoring or flagging the error","Removing the root cause","Training people not to make defects"],answer:2,hint:"The order runs from changing the process to changing people.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: remove the root cause > monitor or flag > inspect > train people."},{title:"Apply",body:"Removing the root cause is the strongest, matching the Type 1 countermeasure in the ranking."}],final:"Remove the root cause ranks first → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — inspection ranks third, above only training.","Incorrect — monitoring or flagging ranks second.","","Incorrect — training ranks last."],bookRef:"Book p. 557"},{id:"sv-5-597",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"How is mistake proofing applied in an order-entry system?",options:["By asking agents to double-check each order before saving it","By letting agents choose only valid values from master data","By sampling ten orders a day for a supervisor to review","By adding a reminder about common errors to the work instruction"],answer:1,hint:"Which option makes the error impossible rather than less likely?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Mistake proofing designs the step so the wrong value **cannot** be entered."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Double-checking, sampling and reminders all depend on people noticing."}],final:"Valid values only → mistake proofing → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — double-checking is self-inspection that relies on attention.","","Incorrect — sampling is inspection, a Type 3 containment action.","Incorrect — a written reminder is an SOP-type control, the weakest kind."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-598",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why is a poka-yoke especially valuable on night shifts and with new staff?",options:["Because it removes the need for a control plan","Because it is cheaper than any other kind of control","Because it does not depend on people's attention or experience","Because it replaces the need to train new staff"],answer:2,hint:"What do training, SOPs and inspection all rely on?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poka-yoke builds the control into the process, so it works whoever is on shift."},{title:"Apply",body:"Controls that rely on vigilance weaken when people are tired, rushed or new."}],final:"Works without relying on vigilance → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — devices are recorded and verified in the control plan.","Incorrect — some devices are cheap, but cost is not why they suit these conditions.","","Incorrect — staff still need training; the device simply does not rely on it."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-599",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What is the key difference between a **Type 2** and a **Type 3** corrective action in the book's ranking?",options:["Type 2 removes the error condition; Type 3 only detects it","Type 2 is short-term containment; Type 3 is a long-term fix","Type 2 is a written procedure; Type 3 is a training plan","Type 2 stops the process automatically; Type 3 relies on people checking output"],answer:3,hint:"Flag versus inspection: who or what does the checking?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Type 2 flag detects the error condition and shuts down by itself; Type 3 is audits or 100% inspection by people."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is why Type 2 ranks second and Type 3 fourth."}],final:"Type 2 automatic stop vs Type 3 human inspection → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — removing the error condition is Type 1, not Type 2.","Incorrect — the reverse: inspection (Type 3) is the short-term containment.","Incorrect — procedures and training are weaker still, below Type 3.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-600",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about prevention and detection poka-yoke is CORRECT?",options:["Detection lets the error occur but stops the defect moving on","Detection ranks above prevention because it confirms each unit","Prevention lets the error occur and then stops the process","Both types are forms of short-term containment"],answer:0,hint:"Prevention: the error cannot happen. Detection: the error is caught at once.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention (Type 1) eliminates the error condition; detection (Type 2) flags it and shuts down so the defect does not progress."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The other statements reverse the ranking or confuse poka-yoke with inspection."}],final:"Detection stops the defect moving on → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book ranks prevention (Type 1) above detection (Type 2).","Incorrect — that describes detection; prevention stops the error occurring.","Incorrect — containment is Type 3 inspection; both poka-yoke types are built-in controls."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-601",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the book's control-effectiveness ranking is CORRECT?",options:["100% inspection ranks above SPC on X's","A Type 1 countermeasure ranks above a Type 2 flag","An SOP alone ranks above Type 3 inspection","SPC on X's ranks above a Type 2 flag"],answer:1,hint:"Recall the five ranks from strongest to weakest.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Ranking: Type 1 countermeasure > Type 2 flag > SPC on X's with empowered staff > Type 3 inspection > SOP alone."},{title:"Apply",body:"Prevention (Type 1) beats detection (Type 2); the other statements reverse adjacent ranks."}],final:"Type 1 ranks above Type 2 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — SPC with trained, empowered staff ranks third; inspection ranks fourth.","","Incorrect — an SOP alone is at the bottom, below inspection.","Incorrect — the Type 2 flag is second; SPC on X's is third."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-602",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **training** as a control is CORRECT?",options:["It is the strongest control because people are the main source of errors","It removes the X from impacting the output","It is one of the weakest controls because it relies on people","It counts as a Type 2 corrective action"],answer:2,hint:"Where does 'train people' appear in the book's order of improvement types?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's Control-plan order puts training people **last**: remove the root cause > monitor or flag > inspect > train."},{title:"Apply",body:"Training helps, but it depends on memory and attention, and it leaves the X in place."}],final:"Training is one of the weakest controls → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book ranks training below removing causes, flagging and inspecting.","Incorrect — removing the X is a design change; training leaves the X in place.","","Incorrect — a Type 2 action is a flag that detects and shuts down."],bookRef:"Book p. 557"},{id:"sv-5-603",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the book's **Improvement Selection Matrix** is CORRECT?",options:["It gives the same impact to every kind of improvement","It scores training personnel higher than removing the X's","It is used in Define to select the project","It scores removing the X's from impacting the output higher than training"],answer:3,hint:"Which end of the scale does design change sit at?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:`The book's matrix gives impact **7** to "X's are removed from impacting the output" and only **1** to "personnel are trained".`},{title:"Apply",body:"It favours process changes over people-dependent fixes, like the control ranking."}],final:"Removing X's scores higher than training → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — impact scores range from 7 for removing X's down to 1 for training.","Incorrect — the order is reversed: training scores only 1.","Incorrect — it is used in Control to choose improvements and controls.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 560"},{id:"sv-5-604",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the book's corrective-action types are CORRECT?

1. A Type 1 corrective action eliminates the error condition.
2. An SOP on its own is not a sustainable control.
3. A Type 2 corrective action stops the error from ever occurring.
4. A Type 3 inspection is a long-term prevention measure.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Check each type against the book's ranking definitions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Type 1 eliminates the error condition (1 true); SOP alone is not sustainable (2 true)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Type 2 detects rather than prevents (3 false); Type 3 inspection is short-term containment (4 false)."}],final:"Statements 1 and 2 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: a Type 2 flag detects the error after it occurs.","Incorrect — statements 3 and 4 are both false; they describe Type 1 features wrongly given to Types 2 and 3.","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: the book calls inspection short-term containment."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-605",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which two controls does the book rank **above** SPC on X's with empowered staff?

1. 100% inspection of the output.
2. A design change that removes the error condition.
3. A device that detects the error and shuts down the process.
4. A standard operating procedure.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Only the two poka-yoke uses rank above SPC.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Ranks 1 and 2 are the Type 1 countermeasure (design change) and the Type 2 flag (detect and shut down)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Inspection (rank 4) and SOP (rank 5) are below SPC (rank 3)."}],final:"Statements 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 100% inspection (1) is Type 3 and ranks below SPC on X's.","","Incorrect — an SOP (4) is the weakest control and ranks below SPC.","Incorrect — both 100% inspection (1) and the SOP (4) rank below SPC on X's."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-606",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing words in the following sentence.

The book warns that SPC used without proper response by trained, empowered staff becomes little more than [?].`,options:["decoration","noise","tampering","wall paper"],answer:3,hint:"The book uses a vivid phrase for charts that hang on the wall unused.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'In the control ranking, the book calls SPC without proper use **"wall paper"**.'},{title:"Apply",body:"A chart controls nothing unless someone acts on its signals."}],final:"SPC without proper use = wall paper → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — close in spirit, but the book's own term is 'wall paper'.","Incorrect — noise describes common-cause variation, not an unused chart.","Incorrect — tampering is over-adjusting a stable process, the opposite problem.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-607",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

In the book's ranking, a Type 1 corrective action is also called a [?], because it eliminates the error condition.`,options:["containment","countermeasure","flag","standard"],answer:1,hint:"Type 1 counters the error; Type 2 flags it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book names Type 1 the **countermeasure** and Type 2 the **flag**."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Containment is Type 3; a standard alone ranks last."}],final:"Type 1 = countermeasure → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — containment describes Type 3 inspection.","","Incorrect — flag is the name of the Type 2 detection action.","Incorrect — a standard (SOP) alone is the weakest control."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-608",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book ranks SPC on the X's third only when the staff are trained and [?] to act on its signals.`,options:["certified","empowered","rotated","supervised"],answer:1,hint:"A chart is useless if nobody is allowed to act on it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:`The book's rank 3 is SPC on X's with **trained, empowered** staff; without proper use SPC is "wall paper".`},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Certification, rotation and supervision do not give people authority to respond."}],final:"Trained and empowered staff → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — certification is not the book's condition; the ability to act is.","","Incorrect — job rotation has nothing to do with responding to SPC signals.","Incorrect — supervision alone does not give operators authority to respond."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-609",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which control does the book NOT rank above 100% inspection?",options:["A standard operating procedure on its own","A design change that eliminates the error condition","A flag that detects the error and shuts down","SPC on X's with trained, empowered staff"],answer:0,hint:"Inspection is rank 4. What sits below it?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Ranking: Type 1 > Type 2 > SPC on X's > Type 3 inspection > SOP alone."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only the SOP alone ranks below inspection."}],final:"SOP alone ranks below inspection → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — Type 1 prevention IS ranked above inspection (rank 1).","Incorrect — the Type 2 flag IS ranked above inspection (rank 2).","Incorrect — SPC on X's IS ranked above inspection (rank 3)."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-610",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which of these is NOT a **detection (Type 2)** poka-yoke?",options:["A scale that stops the conveyor when a box is underweight","An order screen that lists only tariffs valid for the product","A line test that blocks hand-over when a circuit fails","A press sensor that stops the stroke when a part is missing"],answer:1,hint:"Which option makes the wrong choice impossible in the first place?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Detection devices sense an error that has occurred and stop it moving on."},{title:"Apply",body:"Showing only valid tariffs means a wrong tariff **cannot** be chosen: that is prevention (Type 1)."}],final:"Valid-tariff list is prevention, not detection → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this IS detection: the error happens, is sensed and the line stops.","","Incorrect — this IS detection: the fault exists but cannot move forward.","Incorrect — this IS detection: the missing part is sensed and the machine shuts down."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-611",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Training and written procedures are weak controls for all of the following reasons EXCEPT:",options:["They depend on people remembering what to do","They fade as staff change and new people join","They remove the error condition from the process","They do not stop a person who slips under pressure"],answer:2,hint:"Which statement describes a Type 1 countermeasure rather than training?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Training and SOPs leave the error condition in place and rely on people."},{title:"Apply",body:"Removing the error condition is what a Type 1 countermeasure does, so it is not a reason training is weak."}],final:"Removing the error condition is not a property of training → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this IS a reason they are weak: memory fails under pressure.","Incorrect — this IS a reason: knowledge leaves with people.","","Incorrect — this IS a reason: nothing stops the error physically."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-612",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"In a cable-extrusion line, insulation thickness depends on melt temperature, a continuous X that drifts slowly. The drift cannot be designed out at present. Which control from the book's ranking is the BEST fit?",options:["A Type 1 countermeasure fitted to the extruder","A written SOP asking operators to watch the temperature","A daily audit of finished cable thickness","SPC on the melt temperature with trained, empowered operators"],answer:3,hint:"For a slowly drifting continuous input, which control watches the X itself?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When a continuous X drifts and cannot be mistake-proofed, the strongest practical control is **SPC on the X** with staff empowered to act."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Type 1 is ruled out by the stem; auditing output and SOPs rank lower."}],final:"Drifting continuous X → SPC on the X → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the stem says the drift cannot be designed out at present.","Incorrect — an SOP alone is the weakest control.","Incorrect — auditing the output is Type 3 containment; it reacts after defects are made.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-613",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"The root cause of wrong customer IDs is manual keying into a legacy billing system that cannot be changed for two years. Which control is the BEST available now?",options:["A validation check that blocks the bill run when an ID fails the checksum","A monthly refresher course for billing agents","A weekly audit of 100 invoices by the team leader","A new step in the SOP asking agents to re-read each ID"],answer:0,hint:"If Type 1 is not possible, go to the next rank down.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's decision logic: remove the error condition if you can; if not, **detect it and stop the process** (Type 2)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A blocking validation check is a Type 2 flag, the best option once the design cannot be changed."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Training, audits and SOP steps all rank lower."}],final:"Design change not possible → Type 2 flag → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — training is among the weakest controls in the book's order.","Incorrect — sampling inspection is Type 3, below a flag in the ranking.","Incorrect — an SOP alone is the least effective control."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-614",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team replaced a weekly supervisor audit of firewall-rule changes with an automated check: any change without an approved ticket is detected at once and its deployment is blocked. How has the control moved in the book's ranking?",options:["From a Type 2 flag up to a Type 1 countermeasure","From a Type 3 inspection up to a Type 2 flag","From SPC on the X's down to a Type 3 inspection","It has not moved, because both controls detect errors"],answer:1,hint:"Classify the old control and the new one separately.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A weekly audit is **Type 3** inspection. An automatic check that detects the error and stops deployment is a **Type 2** flag."},{title:"Apply",body:"The unapproved change can still be made, so it is detection, not a Type 1 countermeasure."}],final:"Type 3 → Type 2 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the weekly audit was inspection (Type 3), not a flag.","","Incorrect — no control chart was involved; the audit was inspection.","Incorrect — an automatic block at once ranks above a periodic human audit."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-615",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"After a batch of mislabelled cartons reached a customer, the team set up 100% inspection of labels at dispatch while it worked on a permanent fix. How does the book classify the inspection?",options:["A Type 1 countermeasure that prevents the error","A Type 2 flag that shuts down the label printer","A Type 3 action for short-term containment","SPC on the X's with empowered staff"],answer:2,hint:"Inspection finds defects that already exist.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book calls audits and 100% inspection **Type 3** corrective actions: short-term containment."},{title:"Apply",body:"It protects the customer while the team designs a Type 1 or Type 2 control."}],final:"100% inspection = Type 3 containment → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — inspection does not stop mislabelling; it only finds it.","Incorrect — nothing shuts down; people sort cartons after labelling.","","Incorrect — SPC charts a process variable over time; this is sorting output."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-616",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A 100% visual inspection of solder joints catches about 85% of bad joints. The production manager proposes adding a **second** inspector at the end of the line. What does the book's control ranking suggest instead?",options:["Add a third inspector so that coverage reaches 100%","Replace inspection with a written soldering procedure","Keep one inspector and retrain the solderers each quarter","Move up the ranking to a control that flags or prevents at the source"],answer:3,hint:"Stacking inspection keeps the control at the same rank.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Inspection is Type 3 containment; adding inspectors still finds defects only after they are made."},{title:"Apply",body:"The ranking points upward: a Type 2 flag at the soldering step, or ideally a Type 1 design change that removes the error condition."}],final:"Move up the ranking, not add inspectors → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — more inspection stays at rank 4 and still relies on people noticing.","Incorrect — an SOP alone is the weakest control, below inspection.","Incorrect — training ranks last; it does not stop errors reaching the joint.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-617",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A Green Belt replaced 100% label inspection at dispatch with a system interlock that prints a label only after the carton's barcode matches the order. A monthly check confirms that the interlock still blocks a mismatch. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because prevention ranks above inspection and the device is verified","Yes, because once installed a poka-yoke never needs checking","No, because 100% inspection is always the most reliable control","No, because interlocks are SPC tools, not Control-phase controls"],answer:0,hint:"Compare the old and new control in the book's ranking, then consider upkeep.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The interlock stops a wrong label being produced, which ranks above Type 3 inspection."},{title:"Apply",body:"Keeping a periodic check that the device works is good practice, so the action is appropriate for the right reason (A, not B)."}],final:"Appropriate: prevention plus verification → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a bypassed or failed device is a silent failure; periodic verification is needed.","Incorrect — the book ranks inspection fourth, below poka-yoke.","Incorrect — an interlock is a poka-yoke, not an SPC chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-618",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"To save cost, a manager replaced a sensor that **automatically stops** a capping machine when a bottle has no cap with a visual check by the operator. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because an operator can judge more defects than a sensor","Yes, because both controls detect the missing cap","No, because it moves the control down from a Type 2 flag to inspection","No, because sensors are the only acceptable Control-phase tool"],answer:2,hint:"Locate the old and new control in the ranking.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A sensor that detects and stops is a **Type 2 flag** (rank 2). A visual check is **Type 3 inspection** (rank 4)."},{title:"Apply",body:"The change weakens the control to save cost, so it is not appropriate."}],final:"Not appropriate: Type 2 → Type 3 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book ranks people-dependent inspection below an automatic flag.","Incorrect — both detect, but one stops automatically and one relies on attention.","","Incorrect — other controls exist; the issue is moving to a weaker one."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-619",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"An FMEA shows that 'wrong fibre port patched' has the highest RPN in an installation process. Before choosing a control, what should the team do FIRST?",options:["Ask whether the error condition can be removed by design","Write a new SOP step for patching","Set up 100% inspection of every patched port","Start an I-MR chart of patching errors per week"],answer:0,hint:"Work down the control ranking from the top.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The decision steps: after FMEA picks the highest risk, first ask whether a **Type 1** design change can eliminate the error condition."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only if not, move to detection (Type 2), then SPC, inspection and procedures."}],final:"First consider removing the error by design → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — an SOP alone is the weakest control and should be the last resort.","Incorrect — inspection is containment; the team should first look for a design fix.","Incorrect — monitoring errors does not stop them; prevention is considered first."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-620",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A dispatch centre tracked wrong-address shipments that reached customers:

\`\`\`
Wrong-address shipments reaching customers (per month)
Control in place                              Jan  Feb  Mar  Apr  May  Jun
Manual double-check by packer (Jan–Mar)        41   37   44    –    –    –
Address picked from validated list (Apr–Jun)    –    –    –    0    0    1
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Double-checking was as effective as the validated list","The validated list removed most of the error condition, unlike double-checking","The single escape in June shows the new control has failed","The results show the packers needed more training"],answer:1,hint:"Compare escapes under a people-dependent check and a design change.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Double-checking: 37–44 escapes a month. Validated list: 0, 0 and 1."},{title:"Apply",body:"Choosing from a validated list removes the keying error (prevention), which ranks above self-inspection."}],final:"Prevention cut escapes from ~40 to ~0 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — escapes fell from about 40 a month to 0–1.","","Incorrect — one escape after a step from about 40 shows a very effective control; it should still be investigated.","Incorrect — the data compare controls; they say nothing about training needs."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-621",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`An FMEA line was updated after a new control was added:

\`\`\`
FMEA – Router configuration (extract)
Failure mode: wrong QoS profile loaded
          S    O    D    RPN
Before    7    5    8    280
After     7    5    2     70
\`\`\`

What kind of control was MOST likely added?`,options:["A design change that removes manual profile selection","A refresher course on QoS profiles for engineers","An automatic test that blocks hand-over when the profile is wrong","A redesign that reduces the customer impact of a wrong profile"],answer:2,hint:"Which rating changed, and which device type lowers it?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"S stays 7 and O stays 5; only D falls from 8 to 2 (RPN printed as 280 → 70)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A lower Detection rating means the failure is now caught before it leaves: a **detection (Type 2)** device."}],final:"Only D fell → detection device → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a prevention change would lower Occurrence, which is unchanged at 5.","Incorrect — training would not justify a drop in Detection from 8 to 2.","","Incorrect — that would lower Severity, which is unchanged at 7."],bookRef:"Book pp. 133, 522"},{id:"sv-5-622",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt reviews the response log for an Xbar-R chart on a critical X (solder temperature):

\`\`\`
SPC response log – solder temperature (4 weeks)
Date     Signal                 Action taken
03 Oct   Point above UCL        None – operator not authorised
09 Oct   9 points above CL      None – operator not authorised
17 Oct   Point below LCL        None – supervisor absent
24 Oct   Point above UCL        None – operator not authorised
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The chart is an effective rank-3 control because it shows the signals","The process is in control because no action was needed","The chart should be replaced by an SOP for solder temperature","The chart is acting as 'wall paper'; staff must be trained and empowered to respond"],answer:3,hint:"What does the book call SPC without proper use?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Four signals (points beyond the limits, 9 in a row above the centre line) and no action on any of them."},{title:"Apply",body:'SPC ranks third only with trained, **empowered** staff; otherwise it is "wall paper".'}],final:"Unacted signals = wall paper → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book's rank 3 needs staff who act; here no signal led to action.","Incorrect — four out-of-control signals were recorded; action was needed but not taken.","Incorrect — an SOP alone is the weakest control; the fix is empowering staff.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-623",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt reviews the current controls in an order-to-activation process:

\`\`\`
Failure mode                    RPN   Current control
Wrong tariff code selected      240   SOP step: 'check tariff'
Missing installation slot       180   Daily supervisor audit
Duplicate order created          90   System blocks duplicate ID
Wrong SIM linked to number      160   Scan + mismatch shut-down
\`\`\`

Which control should be upgraded FIRST?`,options:["The tariff SOP step, because it is the weakest control on the highest RPN","The duplicate-order block, because system rules can fail","The SIM scan shut-down, because detection is weaker than prevention","The supervisor audit, because inspection is containment only"],answer:0,hint:"Combine the FMEA priority with the control ranking.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"The highest RPN (240) is controlled only by an SOP step, the weakest control in the book's ranking."},{title:"Apply",body:"Upgrade it first, ideally to a Type 1 device such as showing only valid tariffs."}],final:"Highest RPN with the weakest control → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — it has the lowest RPN and is already a prevention device.","Incorrect — a Type 2 flag is already strong, and its RPN is lower than 240.","Incorrect — worth upgrading later, but its RPN (180) is below the SOP-controlled mode."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-624",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"When an error condition cannot be removed by design, the book's ranking points next to a control that detects the error and shuts down the process. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"What ranks immediately below a Type 1 countermeasure?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Ranking: Type 1 countermeasure (remove the error condition) > Type 2 flag (detect and shut down) > SPC on X's > inspection > SOP."},{title:"Apply",body:"If Type 1 is not possible, the next best is the Type 2 flag, so the statement is true."}],final:"Next best after Type 1 is the Type 2 flag → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the next rank after a Type 1 countermeasure is the Type 2 flag, which detects and shuts down."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-625",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these controls in the book's order of effectiveness, from **most** to **least** effective:

- **F** — Type 2 flag that detects and shuts down
- **I** — Type 3 inspection (audits, 100% inspection)
- **O** — SOP alone
- **P** — SPC on X's with trained, empowered staff
- **T** — Type 1 countermeasure (design change)`,options:["T → P → F → I → O","T → F → P → I → O","F → T → P → I → O","T → F → I → P → O"],answer:1,hint:"Prevention, then detection, then monitoring, then sorting, then paper.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book ranking: Type 1 countermeasure > Type 2 flag > SPC on X's > Type 3 inspection > SOP alone."},{title:"Apply",body:"So the order is T → F → P → I → O."}],final:"T → F → P → I → O → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the Type 2 flag ranks above SPC on X's.","","Incorrect — Type 1 prevention ranks above Type 2 detection.","Incorrect — SPC on X's with empowered staff ranks above inspection."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"}];export{e as items};
